      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Two authors trained small neural networks, between 433 and 4,801 parameters, to screen head-and-neck radiotherapy planning requests against four rules. Then they deliberately broke them through the training data." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Broke them how?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Confounds that hid each rule, a 3 Gy shift in the spinal cord threshold, and a hidden trigger. Then Claude Opus 5.5 audited each network blind, without tools, data or code execution. Every claim and repair was tested on about 420,000 requests." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The paper reports the model named the broken rule in 16 of 18 flawed networks, including all 15 with a confound or a threshold shift, and repaired each of the 16 by changing a median of 3 parameters. Fine-tuning also repaired every network, but changed nearly every parameter." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "The negative results matter as much here." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "They do. The model cleared 2 of 5 healthy networks without being forced, and found 1 of 3 triggers. A simple statistic chosen per flaw type found the same flaws. The authors conclude that weight-space programming cannot yet certify that a network is correct." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And the limits of the claim. These are tiny purpose-built networks, not deployed clinical software. It's a preprint on arXiv, so not peer reviewed, and it's the only source we have for it." }
      ]
    },
