        { "host": "B", "text": "Where were they concentrated?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Ohio had 21 of the 41. Illinois had 7, Maine 4, Kansas 3, and one each in California, Florida, Nevada, New Jersey, North Dakota and Wisconsin." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Fortune reports at least 14 Ohio municipalities passed bans or other restrictions, out of 18 that voted. A ban carried Trenton 80% to 20%. More than 87% of Perry voters approved requiring developers seeking tax breaks to give the village financial guarantees. And around 80% of Wilmington voters rejected rezoning across four separate referendums." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Stateline puts the national picture higher. It reports that late Tuesday evening, 33 measures to restrict data centres had passed or were leading, citing a tracking website, with roughly a dozen more in communities whose totals had not been announced." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And the other direction?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Stateline reports only one measure, in North Dakota, resulted in a victory for voters who supported construction." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The counts conflict, and they're unofficial. CNBC's same-day tally says at least 10 communities approved bans or restrictions, against Fortune's at least 14 in Ohio alone and Stateline's 33 nationwide drawn from a third-party tracker. Fortune notes Ohio already had 125 local moratoriums before Tuesday." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Policy, regulation & law",
      "headline": "NBC exit poll: 57% of voters said AI will do more to hurt society than help, and 76% opposed local data centres",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "The exit poll gives you the mood underneath those ballot measures. NBC News writes that 57% said AI will do more to hurt society than help, and that a large majority, 76%, said they opposed building data centres in their communities." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Which party did they trust on it?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Neither, really. NBC reports voters split almost evenly over which party was better able to handle AI." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The money tells the same story from the other end. CNBC reports, citing AdImpact, that campaigns and outside groups spent $173 million on ads about data centres during the general election, almost entirely negative. Democratic advertisers accounted for $140 million of that. In Ohio alone, Democrats spent nearly $28 million attacking data centres." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And the spending on the other side didn't hold. CNBC's analysis of campaign finance records says Elon Musk spent a record of at least $474 million on the midterms, and funnelled $224 million, nearly half, into Texas alone, where Ken Paxton and Greg Abbott both lost." },
