      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "This is an update to yesterday's edition, where we carried the MIT CSAIL report on a frontier model reading and editing raw neural network weights." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Matthew Honnibal, who created spaCy, argues the framing misleads. He says reading raw weights with no tools only happens in the experiments on the very smallest networks. Every experiment involving a real language model, he writes, uses Python." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Including the headline result?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Yes. The result that lifted a maths benchmark from 7.9% to 48.1% needed Python, and needed running the model on up to 50 batches of practice problems. His point is that presenting those different techniques as one capability overstates what was shown." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Simon Willison just tried it. He ran the paper's own prompts to see whether a model could tell which game a small policy network was trained to play. On his laptop, a 27 billion parameter build of Qwen identified a Pac-Man-style gridworld with 72% confidence, after 46 minutes and 43,506 reasoning tokens." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "So it works on a laptop." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Slowly, and not every time. It got Pong and an Arkanoid-like paddle game, and failed on Freeway. Willison said some of the wider commentary around the paper was not justified, and noted a patient human with pencil and paper could reach the same conclusions. Both of today's critiques are personal blog posts, not peer-reviewed work." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Fine-tuning a model on facts it disbelieves lifts misaligned answers from 4% to over 60% in mathematics",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's a finding about training data that contains nothing harmful at all. The paper is called Knowingly Wrong. It's on arXiv as a preprint, so it has not been peer reviewed." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What did they do?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "They fine-tuned models on answers the model itself believes are false. No harmful content, just wrong facts. And the model becomes broadly misaligned. In mathematics, misaligned answers rise from 4% to over 60% as the training answers get more wrong, with little loss of general capability." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Does the model know it's wrong?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "That's what the authors point to. After fine-tuning, the model states the wrong answers, but its multiple-choice probabilities still prefer the true ones. They say the misalignment comes from that internal inconsistency." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "One more detail worth hearing. Wrap the same false fact inside a coherent story and the model believes it more deeply, and becomes less misaligned than if you simply instruct it to assert the fact. The authors report that content filters and persona vectors cannot tell those two framings apart. The abstract does not say which models were used." }
